Subject: kernelbase: Open the calling process for Steam's placeholder PID

Steam's ActiveProcess PID in the registry is the placeholder 0xfffe
until steam.exe writes its real PID, which only happens on launches that
go through steam.exe. Games call OpenProcess on that PID to check that
Steam is alive; open the calling process instead so the check succeeds.

Port of Proton's kernelbase hack.

diff --git a/dlls/kernelbase/process.c b/dlls/kernelbase/process.c
index ae9a7d6..98c9dcf 100644
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/process.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/process.c
@@ -1136,6 +1136,9 @@ HANDLE WINAPI DECLSPEC_HOTPATCH OpenProcess( DWORD access, BOOL inherit, DWORD i
 
     InitializeObjectAttributes( &attr, NULL, inherit ? OBJ_INHERIT : 0, 0, NULL );
 
+    /* Steam ActiveProcess registry PID hack: games query 0xfffe to check if Steam is alive. */
+    if (id == 0xfffe) id = GetCurrentProcessId();
+
     cid.UniqueProcess = ULongToHandle(id);
     cid.UniqueThread  = 0;
 
